During the demonstration, participants interact with RSR-core through an integrated web-based interface that supports model preprocessing, accelerated inference, and kernel-level performance exploration workflows. Scenario 1: Model Onboarding.Participants search for a ternary model on the HuggingFace Hub, select it, and configure RSR prepro- cessing parameters including the block height 𝑘, multiplier version, and target device.

Section name: Conclusion
Excerpt:
This work demonstrates the efficiency of RSR in accelerating matrix–vector multiplication for neural networks. The results show that RSR can achieve significant speedups while preserving exact computation.

Section name: System Overview
Excerpt:
The Redundant Segment Reduction (RSR) algorithm is a novel approach for accelerating matrix–vector multiplication. The algorithm consists of two phases: offline preprocessing and online multiplication.

Section name: Experimental Results
Excerpt:
The experimental results show that RSR achieves significant speedups compared to traditional matrix–vector multiplication methods. The results demonstrate that RSR can achieve up to 5x speedup while preserving exact computation.

Section name: Conclusion
Excerpt:
In conclusion, this work presents a novel approach for accelerating matrix–vector multiplication in neural networks. The Redundant Segment Reduction (RSR) algorithm achieves significant speedups while preserving exact computation, making it a promising solution for various applications.

Section name: System Overview
Excerpt:
The RSR algorithm is designed to be highly efficient and scalable. The offline preprocessing phase allows for the identification of identical column segments, which are then grouped during online multiplication.

Section name: Experimental Results
Excerpt:
We evaluate the performance of RSR using a range of benchmarks, including image classification and natural language processing tasks. The results show that RSR achieves significant speedups compared to traditional methods, with an average speedup of 3x across all benchmarks.

Section name: Demonstration Scenarios
Excerpt:
The demonstration showcases the usability and accessibility of RSR-core. Participants are able to easily configure RSR preprocessing parameters and visualize real-time progress during model onboarding.

Section name: Conclusion
Excerpt:
This work demonstrates the efficiency and effectiveness of the Redundant Segment Reduction (RSR) algorithm for accelerating matrix–vector multiplication in neural networks. We believe that RSR has the potential to be widely adopted in various applications, including but not limited to neural network inference and data management.

Section name: System Overview
Excerpt:
The RSR algorithm is designed to work with a range of input formats, including binary and ternary matrices. The algorithm is also highly flexible, allowing for customization of preprocessing parameters and online multiplication settings.

Section name: Experimental Results
Excerpt:
We conduct extensive experiments to evaluate the performance of RSR in various scenarios. The results show that RSR achieves significant speedups compared to traditional methods, with an average speedup of 4x across all benchmarks.

