In conclusion, this work presents a novel approach for accelerating matrix–vector multiplication in neural networks. The Redundant Segment Reduction (RSR) algorithm achieves significant speedups while preserving exact computation. We believe that this approach has the potential to be widely adopted in various applications.

Section name: System Overview
Excerpt:
The RSR algorithm is designed to accelerate matrix–vector multiplication by reducing redundant computations. By identifying identical column segments and grouping them, the algorithm reduces the number of multiplications required during online multiplication. This allows for efficient computation while preserving exact results.

Section name: Demonstration Scenarios
Excerpt:
During the demonstration, participants interact with RSR-core through an integrated web-based interface that supports model preprocessing, accelerated inference, and kernel-level performance exploration workflows. The UI displays real-time progress as each layer is processed, making it accessible to non-experts.

Section name: Experimental Results
Excerpt:
The experimental results show that RSR achieves significant speedups compared to traditional matrix–vector multiplication methods. The results demonstrate that RSR can achieve up to 5x speedup on a single device while preserving exact computation. We also observe that RSR is highly scalable and can be applied to large neural networks.

